<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agisymba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agisymba was a land in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, known from Ptolemy's Geography, which drew on the earlier geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinus-of-tyre">Marinus of Tyre</a>. According to that account, the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-maternus">Julius Maternus</a> reached it with the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> after four months' travel in the late first century CE. Its location is uncertain; suggestions include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/air-mountains">Aïr Mountains</a> and the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agisymba.jpg" alt="Agisymba" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 48f · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sub-Saharan_Roman_expeditions-explorations.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
